2. Data model: layers and annotations
Persist annotations in the existing per-job tree state, not in a new database model.
Use two logical collections:
annotation_layers
clade_annotations
The exact JSON shape may be adjusted to match existing conventions, but it should be conceptually similar to:
{
  "annotation_layers": [
    {
      "id": "layer_subsections",
      "name": "Subsections",
      "order": 1,
      "visible": true,
      "default_font_family": "Arial",
      "default_font_size": 14,
      "default_font_style": "normal",
      "default_font_weight": "normal",
      "default_text_color": "#222222",
      "default_line_color": "#222222"
    }
  ],
  "clade_annotations": [
    {
      "id": "annotation_a1b2c3",
      "layer_id": "layer_subsections",
      "label": "Subsection A",
      "member_tip_ids": [
        "stable-canonical-leaf-id-1",
        "stable-canonical-leaf-id-2"
      ],
      "font_family": null,
      "font_size": null,
      "font_style": null,
      "font_weight": null,
      "text_color": null,
      "line_color": null
    }
  ]
}
Layer order semantics
This must be unambiguous:
order: 1 is the innermost visible annotation layer, closest to the tree tips.
Higher order values are progressively farther to the right.
Example:
Subsections = order 1
Sections = order 2
Subgenera = order 3
Normalize layer order server-side after saves so it remains deterministic and gap-free where practical.
Layer inheritance
Each layer defines defaults for:
font family
font size
font style: normal or italic
font weight: normal or bold
text color
line color
visible/hidden state
Annotations normally inherit their layer's style.
A per-annotation style field of null or missing means inherit from the layer.
Do not copy every layer default into every annotation. Changing a layer default should immediately affect annotations that have no override for that property.
Backward compatibility
Old tree state without annotation fields must continue to load normally.
Missing annotation fields mean:
no annotation layers
no annotations
Do not rewrite unrelated tree state merely because the viewer loaded.
No database migration should be needed.
3. Canonical leaf identity and duplicate tip names
This is the most important technical requirement.
Annotations must identify biological leaves, never SVG coordinates and never mutable display labels.
Do not persist:
x/y coordinates
current rendered positions
current tree orientation
renamed display labels as the sole identity
Required identity behavior
Inspect the current tree representation and use the strongest stable per-leaf identity already available.
Preferred order:
an existing stable internal/canonical leaf ID, if the viewer/tree state already has one
an existing internal sequence ID that remains stable through display renames and rotations
if neither exists, create a deterministic unique leaf key from immutable tree/input data
A plain canonical name is acceptable only if the current tree model guarantees it is unique.
If duplicate canonical/original names can occur:
do not map membership by name alone
do not silently bind an annotation to the first matching leaf
use or create a stable unique per-leaf key
preserve enough information to match the same biological leaf after reload
The UI may display renamed labels, but persisted annotation membership must remain tied to stable canonical leaf identity.
A stable internal-node ID may be stored as a convenience/hint, but it must not be the authoritative identity of an annotation. Internal-node identity can change after pruning or topology changes.
The authoritative identity is the set of stable member leaf IDs.
4. User workflow
Primary workflow: right-click an internal clade
Extend the existing internal-node right-click menu with:
Add clade annotation…
Do this through the existing node-menu integration rather than creating a second context-menu system.
When selected:
collect all descendant leaf IDs for that internal node
open the annotation editor
let the user enter a label and choose a layer
save the annotation
The user should not have to manually select every descendant of an obvious clade.
Secondary workflow: annotate current selection
Also provide:
Annotate selected clade…
from the Annotation Manager or another unobtrusive existing selection action.
Only allow this if the currently selected leaves correspond exactly to one clade in the current topology.
If they do not, show a clear message such as:
The selected sequences do not form a single clade. Select a complete clade or right-click its branch.
Do not create a misleading bracket spanning unrelated/intervening tips.
Same-layer nesting
Nested annotations within the same layer are allowed.
For example, a user may intentionally place both a broad informal clade and a narrower subgroup in one custom layer.
Do not reject this in the editor.
The renderer must place overlapping/nested annotations in sub-lanes within that layer so their bars/text do not draw directly on top of each other.
Do not warn merely because same-layer annotations are nested.
You may reject or warn about an exact duplicate annotation that uses the same layer and identical member leaf set if the current UX makes that useful, but do not make this a blocker.
