7. SVG rendering and layout
Render annotations inside the tree SVG, preferably in a dedicated group such as:
<g class="clade-annotations">
Do not implement them as absolutely positioned HTML overlays outside the tree.
The target is the existing rectangular tree layout only.
Geometry per annotation
On each relevant redraw:
build a map of stable leaf ID -> current rendered leaf/node position
resolve the annotation's currently visible members
verify the member set still forms one valid clade in the current topology
determine topmost and bottommost Y coordinates
determine the current right edge of rendered tip labels
determine the X position of the annotation's layer and any required sub-lane
draw a thin vertical line spanning the clade
draw the text vertically centered beside the line
Target visual style:
A ─────────────────
B ──────────────
C ──────────────       │
D ──────────────       │  Galerina
E ──────────────       │
F ──────────────       │
A clean vertical line without large decorative caps is the default.
Small end caps are acceptable only if they materially improve readability.
Dynamic layer widths
Do not assume fixed 80 px layer widths.
Measure the currently rendered annotation text and allocate enough horizontal width for each visible layer's longest label plus padding.
Conceptually:
tree labels
    |
    + gap
    |
    | layer order 1
    |  Subsection Foo
    |
    + measured width + gap
    |
    | layer order 2
    |  Section Something Longer
    |
    + measured width + gap
    |
    | layer order 3
The SVG/tree canvas should expand as necessary.
Do not solve clipping by shrinking the biological tree until it becomes unreadable.
Same-layer collision handling
If two annotations in the same layer have overlapping vertical extents, including nested spans:
place them in small horizontal sub-lanes within that layer
use a simple greedy interval-packing strategy
measure resulting width correctly
Do not build an elaborate general graph-layout engine.
Layer visibility
Hidden layers:
do not render bars or labels
do not consume horizontal layout width
remain persisted
do not delete annotations
One-tip annotation
If pruning leaves one member:
keep the annotation
render a short tick/line beside that tip with its label, or another clear single-tip treatment
do not silently delete user-authored annotation text
8. Tree edits and topology changes
Annotations must be recomputed from current tree geometry after:
node rotation
zoom/pan where relevant to SVG transform behavior
viewer resize
tree spacing changes
tip renaming
pruning
rerooting
reload
recompute/topology replacement where appropriate
Do not try to incrementally move persisted annotation coordinates. There should be no persisted coordinates.
Rename
Renaming a displayed tip must not modify or break annotation membership.
Membership remains bound to the stable leaf identity.
Rotation
Node rotation must not change membership.
Only rerender positions.
Pruning
Integrate annotation cleanup into existing pruning state logic.
When a leaf is pruned:
remove its stable leaf ID from every annotation that contains it
preserve remaining members
delete an annotation only when zero members remain
keep an annotation when one member remains
Implement this in a focused helper analogous to other persistent tip-set cleanup rather than scattering annotation cleanup through frontend code.
Rerooting and clade validity
Rerooting must not delete annotations.
After rerooting, determine whether each annotation's saved member set still corresponds exactly to the descendant leaf set of one current node.
If yes:
render normally
If no:
keep it persisted
do not draw a continuous bracket that falsely implies intervening taxa belong to it
show a warning in the manager:
No longer forms one clade in the current rooting
The user may then:
edit it
delete it
recreate it from the current topology
Recompute
Treat annotations as user-authored display metadata.
If recompute changes topology:
preserve annotation/layer state where the underlying leaf identities still exist
revalidate clade membership against the new topology
keep invalid-but-still-resolvable annotations persisted and flagged rather than silently deleting them
remove only member leaves that truly no longer exist, following the same principle as pruning where appropriate
Respect existing explicit reset semantics. Do not broaden "reset" behavior just for annotations.
9. Persistence API, authorization, and concurrency
Use a small additive API consistent with existing tree-state endpoints.
A good design is one atomic endpoint:
POST /api/job/<job_id>/tree/annotations
with a body conceptually like:
{
  "layers": [...],
  "annotations": [...]
}
The existing tree-state GET may continue supplying annotation state if that fits the current architecture.
Do not add redundant endpoints without a reason.
Save behavior
On save:
validate job ID
require normal edit permission with existing check_job_access(..., mode="edit")
load current tree state
validate and normalize the complete submitted annotation configuration
replace only annotation-related state
save using existing tree-state helpers
return the normalized saved configuration
Do not partially mutate annotation state if any submitted item is invalid.
Prefer one configuration save over many tiny requests.
Debounce rapid UI changes where appropriate, especially color controls.
Public/view-only behavior
Anyone allowed to view the tree should see annotations.
Only users with edit permission may:
create
edit
delete
reorder layers
change styles
hide/show persisted layers
Do not rely on hidden buttons for authorization.
Server-side edit checks are mandatory.
Two tabs / concurrent edits
Use last-write-wins semantics for v1.
Do not add optimistic locking/version vectors solely for annotations.
However:
each save must be atomic
annotation saves must preserve unrelated current tree-state keys
the frontend must not intentionally overwrite unrelated tree state with a stale full-state snapshot
If an annotation save fails:
do not pretend it succeeded
show a concise error using the existing status mechanism
restore/reload the last known persisted annotation configuration as appropriate
Undo/history
Annotation edits do not need to participate in any tree undo/history system unless the existing system automatically and generically handles all tree-state mutations already.
Do not build a new undo stack for this task.
